The attached image, Figure 11, presents histological sections of injection sites stained with H&E, comparing residual inflammation two weeks after immunization with aluminum hydroxide (AH) adjuvant (left) versus Nano-11 (right). The image clearly shows that AH induces extensive granulomatous inflammation (asterisks) with dense cellular infiltrates within the skeletal muscle (M), while Nano-11 results in significantly less inflammation, characterized by relatively few macrophages arranged in slender cords. This demonstrates that Nano-11 causes a more transient and less persistent inflammatory response than AH, supporting its advantageous safety profile as a vaccine adjuvant.